scope_add — evaluation
=======================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: scope_add (plans/scope_add)

task
----
make a set of library theories visible in the resource listing for
sustained work. this is the evaluation most likely to challenge its
tool's EXISTENCE: reads are never scope-limited (the one scope
rule), so an agent can complete almost any task without ever
touching scope. the question is whether scope earns its keep for
agents at all, or only for human clients browsing resources.

setup
-----
fresh server, default scope (working set only).

prompt
------
"We'll be working with HOL-Library's algebra theories for a while.
Set things up so that the full list of those theories is visible to
you as browsable resources — I want you to be able to enumerate them
without searching from scratch each time — then show me what became
visible."

success
-------
a pattern covering the target theories was added; the shown listing
reflects it (match count, resources/list agreement).

signals
-------
- honest measurement requires this prompt's directness (it nearly
  names the mechanism) — because round-0 probing showed/will show
  whether agents EVER touch scope unprompted in the other 29
  evaluations. record scope-tool usage across ALL eval transcripts;
  that aggregate, not this eval alone, answers the existence
  question.
- pattern shape: what wildcard syntax does the agent try first, and
  does a zero-match pattern (accepted, count 0 — pinned in the
  plan) read as success-with-nothing or confuse it?
- does the agent verify via resources/list or scope_show after
  adding? (the S1 agreement, seen from the client side.)

reflection
----------
- if the cross-eval aggregate shows scope untouched everywhere and
  agents thriving via search_sources + direct reads, the spec
  question is honest: scope tools may be for human clients and
  resource-UI ergonomics, not agents — keep them but stop expecting
  agent usage, and say so in the spec's scope section.
